FP Trax is a much closer competitor than FacilityOS — European, Nordic reference customers (Chalmers, University of Gothenburg, Skandia), publicly priced from €290/month with a 30-day trial. I read all eight product pages and checked each candidate gap against our repo.

Where they have something we genuinely don't

1. Teams and Slack as notification channels. Our notification_channel enum is literally ('email', 'sms') (20260718090000_parcel_notifications.sql). They notify by email, SMS, Teams and Slack. For an office-parcel product where the recipient sits in Teams all day, this is a cheap, high-visibility gap.

2. Employee/recipient portal — and it's in their basic tier, not an add-on. Second competitor in a row. Our final receivers get a notification and have no login of any kind.

3. SSO (OpenID Connect/OAuth via Entra) + MFA. We sync employees from Entra via Graph, but authentication is still email/password + passkeys. No SSO, no MFA. Already flagged as planned in CLAUDE.md for NIS2 — this confirms it's table stakes, not a nice-to-have.

4. Customer-facing REST API + webhooks. They sell an API for ERP/HR/procurement/facilities integration and outbound webhooks on status change. We have inbound integrations (SFTP, email, Entra) and log drains, but nothing a customer's own systems can call or subscribe to.

5. Live smart locker integrations — Flexity, Cleveron, RENZ. PIN/QR access, prebooked slot reservation, direct delivery without booking, returns through the locker, and assets issued via locker with the borrower identified by their collection code. We have a keynius_bank_id column and an in_locker status; nothing calls any locker API. Both competitors have this live and we don't — that's now the clearest single gap in the product.

6. Private Parcels — an entire commercial module we lack. Employees get their personal online shopping delivered to the workplace: own FP Trax account, unique address ID, carriers bulk-drop at one point, pickup code, returns from the same desk. Sold on HR/talent and ESG (fewer failed home deliveries) rather than logistics. It monetises the mailroom you've already digitised.

7. Laundry / workwear & PPE management. Full garment lifecycle from RFID-tag import to retirement: locker bundle assignment, QR/PIN collection proof, return units, HR-driven reassignment when staff change roles, ISO audit reporting, works with in-house or third-party laundries. Nothing comparable on our side.

8. Inventory withdrawal logging with person + department attribution. This is a bigger gap than I expected. They log who took what and when, allocate consumption cost per department, and produce period-filtered consumption reports. Our inventory_items is one row per item whose quantity gets updated in place — there is no movement table at all, so no "who withdrew it", no consumption history, no departmental cost allocation. Handheld goods-in/goods-out/count writes the number and loses the story.

9. RFID on assets. "Works with barcodes, QR codes and RFID tags." We do barcode/QR, plus NFC cards for handover signatures only.

10. Asset lifecycle alerts and financial fields. Notifications when equipment hits a service or upgrade threshold, plus current value, insurance details and proof of purchase. We have purchased_at, purchase_price, warranty_until and asset_documents, and loan-due reminders — but no service/upgrade thresholds, no insurance or current-value tracking.

11. Scheduled reports (their add-on, automated distribution). Ours are on-demand PDF/Word/CSV only. Same gap FacilityOS showed.

12. Hardware as part of the offer. Pre-configured fixed and handheld scanners, desktop and industrial label printers, ruggedised phones/tablets, locker banks, branded yellow routing labels, consumables. We're software-only — fine as a strategy, but it's a procurement answer we currently don't have.

13. GLN scanning. Their app scans QR, barcode and GLN numbers. Relevant for Nordic/EU public-sector buyers; not handled in our scan path.

Parity — don't chase these

Worth stating explicitly because several look like gaps and aren't: batch intake (their "Multi-Shipment bundling" — our BatchItem flow in parcel-receive-form.tsx), offline scanning (LocalStore.kt has a real pending-receive queue), photo + digital signature, authorised-colleague pickup with dual-name recording, letter handling, Entra/HR directory sync, flexible dashboards with filtering, label printing, search/export, and per-role mobile views (their "Mobile Profiles" ≈ our handheld design + TILE_ROLES).

Where we're ahead

- GDPR/NIS2 depth. They're EU and say "compliance ready" and "audit-ready", but nothing approaching our SAR export, retention schedules with purge, anonymize-instead-of-delete RPCs, ROPA, breach register, free-text field register, DPA/subprocessor docs, read-audit and immutable parcel_events.
- AI label reading with provider choice, disclosure gate and the deterministic fold+fuzzy matching layer. FP Trax has no AI intake at all — notable, since FacilityOS does.
- Booking (rooms/vehicles/equipment) and route planning with real map providers.
- NFC/MIFARE card handover; handling classes with proxy-collection and left-at-location rules; condition capture at intake (preset + note + photo).
- Multi-tenant platform layer: per-product/feature entitlements with expiry, impersonation, billing, per-company login-security policy, biometric/passkey login.
- White-labeling depth: label designer, home and handheld design editors, notification templates, per-tenant text overrides on i18n keys.
- Import pipeline with dry-run preview and import log across parcels, assets and inventory — theirs is bulk import only.

The pattern across both competitors

Three things showed up in both analyses and I'd treat them as the real backlog: live locker integration (we have a stub, both of them ship it), a recipient self-service portal (both have it; FP Trax includes it in the base
plan), and scheduled report delivery. Add SSO/MFA and Teams/Slack notifications for FP Trax specifically — those are the ones a Nordic buyer will ask about in the first meeting.

Usual caveat: these are marketing pages, so absence from a page isn't proof of absence from the product. The gaps above are ones I verified against our own code, not just against their copy.
